      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s historic district, covering 1,107 acres, was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2000.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irvington%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Steamboat Museum on King Carter Drive chronicles Chesapeake Bay steamboats that once docked in Irvington.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irvington%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Irvington hosts the Rappahannock River Yacht Club and the Irvington Marina, reflecting its long water‑borne heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irvington%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The original Chesapeake Academy, founded in 1889, now sits as the Hope & Glory Inn on King Carter Drive.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irvington%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Irvington’s name honors early settler John Carter Sr., father of colonial politician King Carter, who owned the nearby deep‑water confluence.",
